            "title": "Alibaba’s T-Head misses prime IPO window as chip valuations return to rationality",
            "description": "<p><img src=\"https://thebambooworks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/b6debc93e4ec11e7351d790aa159a4af-e1787230267141-900x600-1-500x280.webp\" width=\"500\" alt=\"Chip valuations return to rationality; T-Head misses prime IPO window Alibaba Group recently announced plans to spin off T-Head, its chip-design subsidiary, for an independent public listing in Hong Kong Key Takeaways:  Alibaba submitted its application to the Hong Kong Stock Exchange via a confidential filing  The market estimates T-Head will raise between $2 billion and $3 billion By Lau Chi Hang The Chinese name of T-Head, “PingTouGe,” is a popular moniker for the honey badger, recognized by Guinness World Records as the world's most fearless animal. Despite its diminutive size, the creature boasts a robust physical build and a distinctively fierce temperament. It battles massive predators without a trace of fear, and its extraordinary immunity to venom prompts even deadly snakes to give it a wide berth. After Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. (9988.HK; BABA.US) successfully acquired integrated-circuit design firm C-SKY Microsystems in 2018, it merged the company with the chip team at its DAMO Academy to form a new semiconductor entity. At the time, the team intended to name it “Hummingbird.” However, Jack Ma had just returned from a trip to Africa, where the honey badger's tenacious character had left a lasting impression on him. Overriding all other opinions, he had the final word and christened the new company T-Head. According to Alibaba's announcement, it submitted a confidential application to the Hong Kong Stock Exchange in September this year to spin off and list T-Head (Shanghai) Technology Co. Ltd.. The company stated that the spinoff would better reflect T-Head's intrinsic value and allow investors to more easily assess Alibaba's core businesses. Concurrently, T-Head could attract investors specifically focused on the sector and enhance its overall value, which would facilitate more streamlined and convenient financing efforts should the need arise in the future. T-Head's product portfolio is primarily divided into three categories: First, the Zhenwu series of AI chips, which are used for artificial intelligence model training and inference; second, the Yitian series of server CPUs, primarily designed for use in cloud servers and data centers; and third, Xuantie, which mainly provides RISC-V processor core designs and related IP. Annualized revenue exceeds 10 billion yuan Because Alibaba filed its application confidentially, it did not disclose specific operational or financial data. However, during an analyst call early this year, CEO Eddie Wu Yongming offered a glimpse into the unit's operational status. He noted that as of the end of February this year, cumulative deliveries of chips produced by T-Head had topped 470,000 units, pushing its annualized revenue above 10 billion yuan ($1.45 billion). By May, Gao Hui, vice president of T-Head Semiconductor, further disclosed that as of the end of April, cumulative shipments of the Zhenwu series AI chips had reached 560,000 units, serving more than 400 clients across over 20 industries. By August, the Zhenwu series chips had achieved commercial application via Alibaba Cloud among 650 clients in more than 20 sectors — including autonomous driving, the internet, and finance. According to media reports, T-Head is planning a Hong Kong IPO next year and could raise between $2 billion and $3 billion. However, Alibaba has not yet disclosed the specific offering size or listing timeline. Unlocking valuation and facilitating financing Although Alibaba's share price dipped slightly instead of rising following the announcement of the T-Head spinoff, the move should bring more pros than cons for the group over the long term. First and foremost, it will unlock the value of its semiconductor business. Previously, T-Head was largely viewed as just one piece of the Alibaba Cloud puzzle, making it challenging for the market to evaluate. Investors typically appraised it through the traditional valuation framework applied to the parent company, leaving the chip unit significantly undervalued. After the spinoff, based on current market valuations for AI chip firms — even considering the recent sharp pullback from their peaks — T-Head's valuation will still command a premium over that of a traditional internet platform. With Alibaba retaining a majority stake, unlocking T-Head's massive valuation through an IPO will undoubtedly serve the parent company's best interests. Moreover, going public will pave the way for T-Head's future financing endeavors. After all, the research, development, and iterative upgrades of microchips require staggering amounts of capital. Having a publicly listed platform will allow the company to tap into both equity and debt markets and secure bank credit facilities, all of which will streamline its fundraising and borrowing processes. Remaining tucked under Alibaba's wing would force the parent group to continually inject capital, eventually turning T-Head into a financial burden. Strengthening market image In the past, T-Head's chips were designed exclusively to serve Alibaba. As its operations have grown, it has started catering to external clients. Nevertheless, market watchers continue to view it fundamentally as an in-house supplier for its parent company, raising doubts over whether it provides its absolute best products to outsiders and whether it genuinely puts clients first. Operating with relative autonomy post-IPO — backed by its own management team and institutional investors — will help shake off the stigma of being a single-client servant. This newfound independence will reinforce T-Head's relationships with its clients and suppliers while deepening market trust in its brand. Earlier this year, JPMorgan Chase estimated T-Head's potential valuation at $25 billion to $62 billion. However, with the share prices of chipmakers trading at steep discounts compared with the first half of the year, T-Head has missed the prime IPO window. If calculated based on the recent price-to-sales ratios of Moore Threads (688795.SH) and MetaX (688802.SH) — which range from 40 to 70 times — and factoring in Alibaba's estimate of over 10 billion yuan in revenue for T-Head this year, applying a relatively conservative price-to-sales multiple would place T-Head's market capitalization at roughly $60 billion. To subscribe to Bamboo Works weekly free newsletter, click here\" class=\"wp-post-image\" height=\"280\">The e-commerce giant said it filed confidentially to separately list its chip-design subsidiary in Hong Kong Key Takeaways: By Lau Chi Hang The Chinese name for Alibaba’s T-Head chip unit, Pingtouge, is popular slang for the honey badger, recognized by Guinness World Records as the world’s most fearless animal. Despite its diminutive size, the creature.....</p>",
